From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 11:49:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] i386-gen: -m16 code, GCC's regparm argument passing, inline
 struct copies

-m16 (GCC's -m16, GNU as .code16gcc): the generated code is the 32-bit
code with the 0x66 prefix on every 32-bit operation (including push/pop/
call/ret/jmp/jcc/leave), the 0x67 prefix on every memory operand and no
0x66 on 16-bit operations; the prolog grows by its three prefixes.
Inline asm pushes/pops of operand registers and the flag-output movzbl
get their prefixes too (i386-asm.c).

regparm(N) / -mregparm=N follow GCC's assignment: the arguments are taken
in order, an integer, pointer, long long or structure argument of w
dwords goes into the next w of %eax, %edx, %ecx while enough remain, the
first argument that does not fit and everything after it goes on the
stack, floating point arguments are on the stack and take no register;
a structure return pointer is the first argument (in %eax, not popped
by the callee); variadic functions are left on the stack (tccgen, the
-mregparm default).  Indirect calls of regparm functions go through
memory ("call *disp(%ebp)"), since the three allocatable registers all
carry arguments.

Structure assignments are copied inline with rep movsl (saving %esi,
%edi and %ecx around it) instead of calling memmove: the boot code has
no libc.

Verified: tests/m16-check.sh codegen (the 20 arch/x86/boot/*.c files
compiled with -m16 and -m32 disassemble to the same instruction
streams) and regparm (gcc-compiled and tcc-compiled callers/callees
with 12 argument-passing cases call each other correctly in 32-bit
static binaries); make test passes.
